<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lutheranism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lutheran</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lutheranism is the branch of Protestant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> based on the teachings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>, who launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in 1517. Its spread divided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> along religious lines and led to wars ended by settlements such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-augsburg">Peace of Augsburg</a>. Lutheranism became the state religion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a> and gained followers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lutheranism.jpg" alt="Lutheranism" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Atleett · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Konsekration_Alsike_Kyrka.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Unlike their Franconian cousins, who adopted <b>Lutheranism</b>, the Swabian counts and their subjects remained Catholic through the Reformation, and the county lay within the diocese of Constance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hohenzollern">County of Hohenzollern · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dutch Reformed Church was the public church, but Catholics, Mennonites, <b>Lutherans</b> and Jews could worship privately, and Sephardic Jews from Portugal settled in Amsterdam.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Otto Henry introduced <b>Lutheranism</b> in 1556, and Frederick III turned to Reformed Protestantism, ordering images removed from churches and imposing strict church discipline.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Peace of Augsburg in 1555 allowed territorial rulers to choose between Catholicism and <b>Lutheranism</b> for their domains, and the Peace of Westphalia in 1648 extended recognition to Calvinism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Religion shaped society deeply: the Hussite revolution transferred much church land to nobles and towns, and for two centuries Utraquists, who gave communion in both kinds, coexisted with Catholics, later joined by the Unity of the Brethren and <b>Lutherans</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Warsaw Confederation of 1573 guaranteed peace among the nobility’s Catholics, <b>Lutherans</b>, Calvinists, Orthodox and other confessions at a time when the St. Bartholomew's Day Massacre in France and the Duke of Alba’s repression in the Netherlands showed the murderous alternative.</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
